1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:07,870
Listen.

2
00:00:08,530 --> 00:00:09,050
Practice.

3
00:00:09,570 --> 00:00:09,910
Speak.

4
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:16,200
Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:16,270 --> 00:00:16,460
Yeah.

6
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:19,710
Think of it like eavesdropping, but for learning.

7
00:00:20,130 --> 00:00:22,190
It's the actual day to day language people are using.

8
00:00:22,310 --> 00:00:22,610
Right.

9
00:00:22,790 --> 00:00:23,830
Not textbook stuff.

10
00:00:23,890 --> 00:00:26,480
And our mission here is to sift through this together.

11
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:33,140
We want to highlight those essential phrases, the patterns that keep business talk clear, polite and ultimately productive.

12
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:34,690
And why should you care?

13
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:39,750
Well, mastering these everyday exchanges, even the simple ones, it unlocks smoother interactions.

14
00:00:39,790 --> 00:00:44,200
It helps you get things done, feel more confident in, well, all sorts of professional situations.

15
00:00:44,650 --> 00:00:49,200
How it underlines the importance of just foundational language.

16
00:00:49,220 --> 00:00:54,860
It shows that no matter how complex your job is, there's this core set of like communication tools you need.

17
00:00:54,960 --> 00:00:55,720
It's the bedrock.

18
00:00:55,890 --> 00:00:57,290
Okay, so let's get into it.

19
00:00:57,830 --> 00:01:01,550
The first big theme that jumps out is just starting the conversation, you know?

20
00:01:02,020 --> 00:01:04,680
Initiating contact and building a little bit of rapport.

21
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:06,020
Yeah, the simple stuff.

22
00:01:06,460 --> 00:01:09,050
Easy to overlook, but it really sets the tone.

23
00:01:09,430 --> 00:01:12,970
Just full of examples like, good morning or nice to see you.

24
00:01:13,010 --> 00:01:13,870
How's your day going?

25
00:01:13,890 --> 00:01:14,710
How was the weekend?

26
00:01:14,770 --> 00:01:15,450
That kind of thing.

27
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:16,560
Exactly.

28
00:01:16,780 --> 00:01:18,560
And the replies are just as important.

29
00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:20,900
Things like, likewise, always a pleasure.

30
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:24,750
Or, you know, the standard, doing well, thanks, how about you?

31
00:01:24,890 --> 00:01:25,140
Yeah.

32
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:26,600
Or even just acknowledging the weather.

33
00:01:26,660 --> 00:01:26,840
Right.

34
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:27,920
Beautiful day out.

35
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:28,760
Absolutely.

36
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:29,580
Little things.

37
00:01:29,610 --> 00:01:31,650
And these aren't just like wasted moments.

38
00:01:31,690 --> 00:01:32,370
They're crucial.

39
00:01:32,510 --> 00:01:35,000
They build that connection, make people feel seen.

40
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:38,770
You know, it makes the actual work talk go smoother afterwards.

41
00:01:39,110 --> 00:01:40,290
It signals respect.

42
00:01:40,270 --> 00:01:41,090
Totally agree.

43
00:01:41,130 --> 00:01:42,330
It greases the wheels.

44
00:01:43,110 --> 00:01:50,370
Okay, so after the hellos, a massive part of workplace chat is meeting something, making requests, asking for help.

45
00:01:50,550 --> 00:01:50,970
Oh, yeah.

46
00:01:51,490 --> 00:01:54,330
And the source gives a great overview of how people do this clearly.

47
00:01:54,350 --> 00:01:57,520
You see the direct approach like, can you send me the report by tomorrow?

48
00:01:57,680 --> 00:01:58,020
Right.

49
00:01:58,220 --> 00:02:02,130
And then maybe slightly softer ways, like, may I have a moment of your time?

50
00:02:03,310 --> 00:02:04,670
And then there are all the practical needs.

51
00:02:04,890 --> 00:02:05,110
Yeah.

52
00:02:05,130 --> 00:02:06,190
Can I borrow your stapler?

53
00:02:06,520 --> 00:02:07,280
I need the printer.

54
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:08,680
Or asking for information.

55
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:10,060
Can you tell me about your background?

56
00:02:10,180 --> 00:02:11,400
Can you give me directions?

57
00:02:11,720 --> 00:02:13,510
Or help with a task.

58
00:02:13,610 --> 00:02:14,490
Can you review this?

59
00:02:14,930 --> 00:02:16,150
Need to talk to the manager.

60
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:20,470
And specific operational stuff, too, like, can you please transfer inventory?

61
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:20,860
Yeah.

62
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:22,880
Or do an inventory count.

63
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:25,600
The word please shows up a lot.

64
00:02:25,620 --> 00:02:26,250
Always polite.

65
00:02:26,530 --> 00:02:27,750
Yeah.

66
00:02:27,770 --> 00:02:30,670
And even offering help is framed as a request, isn't it?

67
00:02:30,750 --> 00:02:30,990
Yeah.

68
00:02:31,010 --> 00:02:32,360
Is there anything you need help with?

69
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:34,180
You're asking if you can assist.

70
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:35,000
Good point.

71
00:02:35,020 --> 00:02:36,600
So the takeaway is really about clarity.

72
00:02:37,030 --> 00:02:42,210
Using clear phrases like can you or may I just cuts through ambiguity.

73
00:02:42,690 --> 00:02:43,640
People know what you need.

74
00:02:43,870 --> 00:02:47,850
Which leads right into the flip side, how people respond to those requests.

75
00:02:47,870 --> 00:02:51,200
And this source material, it's packed with examples, a whole spectrum.

76
00:02:51,340 --> 00:02:51,680
Definitely.

77
00:02:51,700 --> 00:02:53,060
You get the immediate yes answers.

78
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:53,680
Absolutely.

79
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:54,060
Here it is.

80
00:02:54,420 --> 00:02:55,460
Of course, I'll do that.

81
00:02:55,480 --> 00:02:56,200
Yeah, that works.

82
00:02:56,220 --> 00:02:58,000
Or the I'll try responses.

83
00:02:58,660 --> 00:03:00,420
I'll do my best to get it to you on time.

84
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:01,080
Right.

85
00:03:01,490 --> 00:03:03,490
And the ones that need a bit more info or flexibility.

86
00:03:03,870 --> 00:03:04,370
No problem.

87
00:03:04,410 --> 00:03:09,390
Just let me know when or let me check the schedule, but it should be OK. We'll see if she's free.

88
00:03:09,610 --> 00:03:11,330
And just accepting help when it's offered.

89
00:03:11,490 --> 00:03:11,790
Thank you.

90
00:03:11,810 --> 00:03:12,530
I could use some help.

91
00:03:12,570 --> 00:03:13,090
That would be great.

92
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:13,680
Thanks.

93
00:03:13,770 --> 00:03:17,600
Even managing expectations around sharing things like, sure, just bring it back when you're done.

94
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:18,520
Simple stuff.

95
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:19,400
Yeah.

96
00:03:19,420 --> 00:03:25,840
But what I found really useful were the examples of saying no or offering alternatives, you know, gracefully.

97
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:28,220
It's not just a flat no.

98
00:03:28,260 --> 00:03:30,000
It's like, sorry, I have another commitment.

99
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:34,950
And sometimes adding a suggestion, maybe try a cap or declining an invite.

100
00:03:35,590 --> 00:03:37,320
Thanks, but I can't make it.

101
00:03:37,530 --> 00:03:38,880
And for bigger things, too.

102
00:03:39,240 --> 00:03:43,140
Like, if a proposal isn't workable, sorry, but that's not feasible for us.

103
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:47,490
And the response isn't defensive, it's, okay, let's explore other options then.

104
00:03:48,120 --> 00:03:48,870
Problem solving.

105
00:03:49,010 --> 00:03:49,430
Exactly.

106
00:03:49,870 --> 00:03:52,210
Or needing to escalate, I need to check with my manager.

107
00:03:52,930 --> 00:03:54,290
And the reply is understanding.

108
00:03:54,950 --> 00:03:55,870
Understood, let me know.

109
00:03:56,460 --> 00:03:59,020
So being responsive isn't just about saying yes.

110
00:03:59,220 --> 00:03:59,760
Not at all.

111
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:06,610
It's about acknowledging, managing expectations, saying if it's possible, and often suggesting what's next.

112
00:04:07,150 --> 00:04:11,430
Even when you decline, doing it this way builds trust, keeps the conversation going.

113
00:04:12,130 --> 00:04:12,510
Makes sense.

114
00:04:12,950 --> 00:04:14,030
Okay, shifting gears a bit.

115
00:04:14,510 --> 00:04:17,320
Scheduling, constant back and forth in any office.

116
00:04:17,580 --> 00:04:18,200
Oh, absolutely.

117
00:04:18,660 --> 00:04:22,180
The source shows the language is all about being clear and getting confirmation.

118
00:04:22,860 --> 00:04:24,730
Proposing times, can we meet at 10?

119
00:04:25,230 --> 00:04:27,790
Let's do 2 p.m. How about a call tomorrow?

120
00:04:27,900 --> 00:04:30,530
Or needing to change plans, need to reschedule.

121
00:04:30,810 --> 00:04:32,650
Let's touch base later this afternoon.

122
00:04:32,690 --> 00:04:33,090
Right.

123
00:04:33,330 --> 00:04:35,480
And then confirming what time is the meeting again.

124
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:37,720
And the source shows these really simple lock-ins.

125
00:04:38,160 --> 00:04:40,220
Available Tuesday, 10 a.m. That works.

126
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:40,900
Let's lock it in.

127
00:04:41,260 --> 00:04:42,730
Or can we schedule for Tuesday?

128
00:04:43,230 --> 00:04:44,170
Sure, I'll pencil that in.

129
00:04:44,290 --> 00:04:45,950
You even see people looking forward to meetings.

130
00:04:46,110 --> 00:04:47,490
Looking forward to our meeting tomorrow.

131
00:04:47,550 --> 00:04:48,050
Me too.

132
00:04:48,250 --> 00:04:48,810
Should be good.

133
00:04:49,290 --> 00:04:49,390
Yeah.

134
00:04:49,410 --> 00:04:51,040
Or just giving a heads up about being out.

135
00:04:51,420 --> 00:04:52,160
I'll be out for two days.

136
00:04:52,780 --> 00:04:53,040
Okay.

137
00:04:53,180 --> 00:04:54,100
Thanks for the heads up.

138
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:55,800
It seems obvious, but...

139
00:04:55,950 --> 00:04:59,260
Using precise language for scheduling saves so much hassle, right?

140
00:04:59,640 --> 00:05:01,680
Avoids double bookings, missed meetings.

141
00:05:02,140 --> 00:05:03,420
It's basic coordination.

142
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:04,080
Totally.

143
00:05:04,750 --> 00:05:06,170
Clear communication makes it work.

144
00:05:07,030 --> 00:05:11,460
And once things are scheduled, the talk turns to, well, how things are going.

145
00:05:12,140 --> 00:05:13,640
Progress problems.

146
00:05:13,660 --> 00:05:16,120
Yeah, discussing projects, progress, concerns.

147
00:05:16,260 --> 00:05:17,000
That's constant.

148
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:19,850
The examples show how to ask for updates efficiently.

149
00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:22,530
Direct asks, can you give me an update on the project?

150
00:05:23,130 --> 00:05:24,450
And specific replies.

151
00:05:24,750 --> 00:05:26,470
We're about 70% done.

152
00:05:27,110 --> 00:05:28,830
Or linking it to bigger goals.

153
00:05:28,850 --> 00:05:31,000
We've hit 75% of our quarterly goals.

154
00:05:31,260 --> 00:05:33,040
And you see the positive reinforcement too.

155
00:05:33,080 --> 00:05:34,140
Excellent progress.

156
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:34,660
Keep it up.

157
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:36,660
But also talking deadlines.

158
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:37,620
What's the deadline?

159
00:05:38,140 --> 00:05:39,290
Submission is the 15th.

160
00:05:39,710 --> 00:05:41,790
And managing expectations if things are tight.

161
00:05:42,190 --> 00:05:43,250
Can you finish by Friday?

162
00:05:43,770 --> 00:05:47,360
Followed by a realistic, I'll try my best, but might need a bit more time.

163
00:05:47,620 --> 00:05:53,390
What I thought was really valuable here was the language for raising concerns before they become disasters.

164
00:05:53,430 --> 00:05:53,670
Yes.

165
00:05:54,110 --> 00:05:54,630
Proactive.

166
00:05:54,960 --> 00:05:58,320
Things like I'm concerned about the deadline or I'm worried we're not on track.

167
00:05:58,360 --> 00:06:00,870
As responses aren't panic, they're action oriented.

168
00:06:00,890 --> 00:06:03,070
OK, let's reevaluate the timeline.

169
00:06:03,190 --> 00:06:05,150
We can regroup and adjust our strategy.

170
00:06:05,330 --> 00:06:06,520
Even tackling resources.

171
00:06:06,740 --> 00:06:10,850
Worried we don't have enough resources, which prompts maybe we can reallocate some.

172
00:06:10,910 --> 00:06:16,460
So the pattern is identify the potential issue using words like concerned or worried.

173
00:06:16,620 --> 00:06:16,840
Right.

174
00:06:16,900 --> 00:06:18,960
And that triggers a problem solving conversation.

175
00:06:19,320 --> 00:06:20,260
Let's reevaluate.

176
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:21,440
Let's discuss solutions.

177
00:06:21,920 --> 00:06:23,550
It's about collaborative troubleshooting.

178
00:06:23,810 --> 00:06:26,890
Moving on to another key area, feedback and opinions.

179
00:06:27,630 --> 00:06:29,390
This is woven all through the source material.

180
00:06:30,070 --> 00:06:30,890
Collaboration.

181
00:06:31,270 --> 00:06:31,860
Improvement.

182
00:06:31,870 --> 00:06:33,230
Yeah, and this is where it gets interesting.

183
00:06:33,490 --> 00:06:35,710
Navigating potentially sensitive stuff.

184
00:06:36,030 --> 00:06:38,180
You see people directly offering input.

185
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:39,260
Can I make a suggestion?

186
00:06:39,780 --> 00:06:42,100
Or got some suggestions if you're open.

187
00:06:42,420 --> 00:06:44,200
And the key is that welcoming response.

188
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:46,530
Of course, always open to feedback.

189
00:06:47,070 --> 00:06:48,470
Yeah, eager to hear your thoughts.

190
00:06:48,690 --> 00:06:50,190
Then sharing the actual feedback.

191
00:06:50,210 --> 00:06:54,000
Some feedback on your presentation met with, okay, please share it.

192
00:06:54,020 --> 00:06:57,770
The source also shows how opinions get stated, suggesting ideas.

193
00:06:57,930 --> 00:06:59,690
I think we need more research response.

194
00:07:00,150 --> 00:07:00,840
Sounds prudent.

195
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:01,560
I'll get more data.

196
00:07:01,960 --> 00:07:03,640
Or stating a stronger view.

197
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:06,550
In my view, this proposal isn't feasible.

198
00:07:07,310 --> 00:07:08,850
But notice it's not an argument ender.

199
00:07:09,010 --> 00:07:09,970
It invites discussion.

200
00:07:10,890 --> 00:07:12,190
OK, why do you think so?

201
00:07:12,410 --> 00:07:12,850
Let's talk.

202
00:07:13,180 --> 00:07:15,120
And people asking for clarification, too.

203
00:07:15,180 --> 00:07:16,480
Did I get that right?

204
00:07:16,620 --> 00:07:18,020
Or can you clarify what you mean?

205
00:07:18,080 --> 00:07:20,570
And the other person clarifying, sorry, I was vague.

206
00:07:21,010 --> 00:07:21,570
Here's what I meant.

207
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,390
Handling disagreement professionally is also covered.

208
00:07:24,970 --> 00:07:31,140
Phrases like, I understand your perspective, but I see it differently, aiming for, let's find middle ground.

209
00:07:31,380 --> 00:07:33,080
Or setting boundaries respectfully.

210
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:35,870
Can we agree to disagree and move on?

211
00:07:36,450 --> 00:07:40,390
Followed by, agreed, let's focus on what's next.

212
00:07:40,810 --> 00:07:45,020
The insight here isn't just what you say when giving feedback or opinions, but how.

213
00:07:45,700 --> 00:07:51,270
It's about being clear, respectful, and framing it as a discussion, not just criticism.

214
00:07:51,650 --> 00:07:54,320
Which brings us nicely to when things don't go right.

215
00:07:54,680 --> 00:07:55,300
Apologies.

216
00:07:55,540 --> 00:07:56,040
They happen.

217
00:07:56,220 --> 00:07:57,380
Inevitable in work life.

218
00:07:57,840 --> 00:07:59,720
And the source shows lots of ways they're handled.

219
00:08:00,080 --> 00:08:01,670
You see, apologies for common things.

220
00:08:01,930 --> 00:08:03,130
Sorry I couldn't finish on time.

221
00:08:03,290 --> 00:08:04,070
Sorry I'm running late.

222
00:08:04,410 --> 00:08:05,410
Sorry if I made a mistake.

223
00:08:05,470 --> 00:08:07,190
Or for bigger things, like tech issues.

224
00:08:07,370 --> 00:08:09,070
Sorry I didn't get your email due to tech problems.

225
00:08:09,270 --> 00:08:12,020
And the responses are key for moving forward.

226
00:08:12,480 --> 00:08:14,100
They often minimize it and look ahead.

227
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:14,760
That's okay.

228
00:08:14,780 --> 00:08:15,680
Let's regroup.

229
00:08:15,700 --> 00:08:16,160
No worries.

230
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:17,390
Happens to the best of us.

231
00:08:17,550 --> 00:08:19,070
Let's fix it.

232
00:08:19,090 --> 00:08:19,290
All right.

233
00:08:19,310 --> 00:08:20,850
Let's just make sure it doesn't happen again.

234
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
There are customer-related apologies, too.

235
00:08:23,280 --> 00:08:24,800
Sorry you're not satisfied with the product.

236
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:28,270
Sorry we're out of stock, which trigger those problem-solving responses.

237
00:08:28,570 --> 00:08:29,470
Let's find a solution.

238
00:08:29,690 --> 00:08:30,770
I'm really sorry about that.

239
00:08:31,170 --> 00:08:31,850
I'll sort it out.

240
00:08:31,990 --> 00:08:32,190
Yeah.

241
00:08:32,650 --> 00:08:36,750
Even asking someone to repeat something can be softened with an apology.

242
00:08:37,480 --> 00:08:38,380
Sorry, could you repeat that?

243
00:08:38,580 --> 00:08:40,860
So effective apologies seem to be clear.

244
00:08:40,920 --> 00:08:43,320
They take responsibility or acknowledge the issue.

245
00:08:44,360 --> 00:08:48,210
And they're often followed by a language focused on fixing it or stopping it from happening again.

246
00:08:48,760 --> 00:08:51,800
And the response, accepting the apology, is just as important.

247
00:08:52,420 --> 00:08:56,910
Okay, circling back to building connections, introductions, getting to know colleagues.

248
00:08:57,030 --> 00:09:00,170
Yeah, the standard ways people introduce themselves or ask about others.

249
00:09:00,530 --> 00:09:01,640
Can you introduce yourself?

250
00:09:02,040 --> 00:09:02,280
Answer.

251
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:05,280
I'm Lisa Smith, project manager at XYZ.

252
00:09:06,040 --> 00:09:07,410
Or asking about background.

253
00:09:07,630 --> 00:09:09,030
Can you tell me more about your background?

254
00:09:09,230 --> 00:09:11,050
And the classic simple exchange.

255
00:09:11,470 --> 00:09:12,500
Hi, I'm Tom.

256
00:09:12,630 --> 00:09:13,460
Nice to meet you, Tom.

257
00:09:13,520 --> 00:09:14,040
I'm Sarah.

258
00:09:14,560 --> 00:09:16,280
Or, slightly more info, nice to meet you.

259
00:09:16,320 --> 00:09:16,940
What do you do?

260
00:09:17,400 --> 00:09:18,150
Project manager.

261
00:09:18,230 --> 00:09:18,710
How about you?

262
00:09:18,750 --> 00:09:21,510
And in groups, how introductions are managed.

263
00:09:22,070 --> 00:09:22,980
Let's go around the room.

264
00:09:23,840 --> 00:09:25,080
And welcoming new people.

265
00:09:25,680 --> 00:09:26,100
Hi, everyone.

266
00:09:26,120 --> 00:09:26,540
I'm John.

267
00:09:26,640 --> 00:09:27,470
Nice to meet you, Jen.

268
00:09:27,570 --> 00:09:28,370
Welcome aboard.

269
00:09:28,450 --> 00:09:30,630
So clear intros are fundamental.

270
00:09:31,390 --> 00:09:34,880
And showing interest in others, what do you do, is key for rapport.

